Justin Florentine (Besu): Okay, let's…

326
00:38:32.640 --> 00:38:44.639
Justin Florentine (Besu): I'll slow down here for a second. We're moving kind of fast, and we're throwing around a lot of ideas here. But the CRS sounds like a very promising area that we should, we should pursue.

327
00:38:44.790 --> 00:38:52.379
Justin Florentine (Besu): If we do, we need to reconsider…

328
00:38:52.890 --> 00:38:55.989
Justin Florentine (Besu): maybe going straight to PQ, right?

329
00:38:56.170 --> 00:39:02.859
Justin Florentine (Besu): it might be too much work to actually try to entertain doing it both pre- and post-quantum.

330
00:39:02.860 --> 00:39:07.480
Benedikt Wagner: post-quantum CRS, I don't know if there's a solution.

331
00:39:07.970 --> 00:39:18.799
Gottfried Herold: Yeah, because the protocols we have, I mean, if you want to make a post-quantum CRS ceremony, you basically, I mean, you basically end up doing a fully-fledged MPC protocol.

332
00:39:18.800 --> 00:39:26.780
Benedikt Wagner: It's also not even clear what the CRS would be and which threshold Encrypt… post-quantum threshold Encryption protocol would use such a CRS.

333
00:39:26.780 --> 00:39:27.800
Gottfried Herold: Yeah, yeah.

334
00:39:28.030 --> 00:39:31.260
Jan Bormet: I think with PQ, there's basically nothing better than a naive scheme right now.

335
00:39:31.780 --> 00:39:32.270
Jan Bormet: Right.

336
00:39:33.670 --> 00:39:34.510
Benedikt Wagner: Yeah, pretty much.

337
00:39:34.510 --> 00:39:35.170
Jan Bormet: Bill?

338
00:39:35.170 --> 00:39:38.379
Gottfried Herold: Yeah, a little bit of things, but not much, you know?

339
00:39:40.650 --> 00:39:46.639
Justin Florentine (Besu): So, is it safe to say that with the naive scheme and post-quantum, it's really a committee scheduling problem?

340
00:39:48.260 --> 00:40:02.880
Justin Florentine (Besu): Like, it's really figuring out who you need to target, how do you break up your shares, how you deliver them, and then they need to decide when to reveal, and the protocol needs to decide how to punish them if they, you know, collude or fail to reveal.

341
00:40:03.380 --> 00:40:07.410
Gottfried Herold: I mean, there's also the problem that the data sizes grow quite a lot.

342
00:40:07.770 --> 00:40:11.019
Gottfried Herold: And, I mean, it's just the scaling of the data complexities.

343
00:40:11.020 --> 00:40:14.420
Justin Florentine (Besu): I mean, they do, but they're linear, we said, right?

344
00:40:16.350 --> 00:40:18.070
Jan Bormet: I mean, linear in the number of parties, right?

345
00:40:18.070 --> 00:40:29.650
Gottfried Herold: number of parties, and you have to… and there's… I mean, if you do it naively, unless you do some threshold IBE scheme, there's also sort of… you have to do that for every… for every encrypted transaction.

346
00:40:30.620 --> 00:40:39.680
Gottfried Herold: And the base size of the individual ciphertex also grows quite a lot. I mean, this linear growth is… that's kind of the problem. Just the concrete numbers are probably gonna be bad.

347
00:40:43.900 --> 00:40:46.420
Justin Florentine (Besu): So it's gonna punish bandwidth pretty hard.

348
00:40:46.630 --> 00:40:47.180
Gottfried Herold: Yeah.

349
00:40:47.280 --> 00:40:49.460
Gottfried Herold: That, yeah, it's mostly a vendor's problem.

350
00:40:52.530 --> 00:40:58.110
Anders Elowsson: Yeah, it would be… we would have to charge the transaction… the senders quite a lot.

351
00:40:59.970 --> 00:41:03.040
Anders Elowsson: You cannot do many per block.

352
00:41:03.810 --> 00:41:20.850
Gottfried Herold: Yeah, it's not just the centers, it's also the problem that the committee, if you sort of select a committee randomly from the validators, and those validators who are selected actually have a much… have a huge bandwidth requirement spike, because they have to basically send out decryptions for every single encrypted trans… every single encrypted Mempool transaction.

353
00:41:20.850 --> 00:41:21.500
Benedikt Wagner: But that's not.

354
00:41:21.500 --> 00:41:22.099
Gottfried Herold: On this one.

355
00:41:22.100 --> 00:41:31.450
Benedikt Wagner: I mean, that's not the main drawback of a naive scheme. This is… this is a drawback that you have with every scheme that is not doing batching.

356
00:41:31.910 --> 00:41:35.360
Gottfried Herold: Yeah, or threshold, yeah, or threshold IP, yeah.

357
00:41:36.570 --> 00:41:48.300
Justin Florentine (Besu): Can we better define what we mean by batching? When I hear batching, I think, okay, everybody that's participating, in maybe this, or wants to participate in this block.

358
00:41:48.500 --> 00:41:55.309
Justin Florentine (Besu): batches their transactions together, and they're all, you know, encrypted with the same key. Is that an oversimplification?

359
00:41:55.310 --> 00:42:03.390
Gottfried Herold: Yeah, it's a bit of a simplification. I mean, this is one way to achieve it. In general, but batching, I mean, more generally, the point is what you want to achieve, is that sort of the…

360
00:42:03.680 --> 00:42:12.389
Gottfried Herold: The bandwidth complexity does not, for the decryption part, actually does not grow linearly with the number of, encrypted transactions.

361
00:42:13.280 --> 00:42:26.430
Jan Bormet: Yeah, it's kind of the opposite, what you said, like, the encryption should be the same. Like, you should not really know… I mean, yeah, you notice because you run a different protocol, but you should not have to coordinate with other encryptors, let's say. But, the… instead of sending, let's say you have

362
00:42:26.830 --> 00:42:42.669
Jan Bormet: B transactions in the ciphertext, and in the normal threshold encryption scheme, if I'm a validator that is scheduled to decrypt, then I need to send, let's say, 100 individual decrypts, like, every single ciphertext, I need to have to send an individual partial decryption.

363
00:42:42.890 --> 00:42:50.990
Jan Bormet: And if it's a batching schema, I can just compress this to one. So it's kind of about the succinctness of the partial decryption when trying to decrypt many transactions at once.

364
00:42:50.990 --> 00:42:55.920
Justin Florentine (Besu): Oh, so it's batching the shares, not so much the participants, okay.

365
00:42:56.330 --> 00:42:56.840
Jan Bormet: Yep.

366
00:43:02.120 --> 00:43:07.319
Justin Florentine (Besu): Okay. Wow, I said we were gonna brainstorm, and you guys gave me quite the storm.

367
00:43:07.380 --> 00:43:23.120
Justin Florentine (Besu): We have a lot to think about. We're coming up on, about 3 quarters of the way through the meeting, and we do have another agenda item that Jannik wanted to cover, so I want to reserve a little bit of time for that. Do you want to get into it, Jannik?

368
00:43:24.460 --> 00:43:27.630
Jannik Luhn: Sure. So basically…

369
00:43:27.720 --> 00:43:37.590
Jannik Luhn: it's motivated by, the fact that I'm not really convinced that enshrining a specific server encryption scheme is,

370
00:43:37.620 --> 00:43:50.119
Jannik Luhn: So the best solution? First of all, because, as we've just discussed, there's still lots of cryptographic development to be had, hopefully, so if we try and find something now, we probably won't end up with the best scheme.

371
00:43:50.310 --> 00:44:00.500
Jannik Luhn: And… but even if we had the best scheme, then, we will probably only be able to have a small committee, sampled from the value dataset, and I'm not sure how much I would…

372
00:44:00.550 --> 00:44:15.069
Jannik Luhn: how trustworthy that actually is, especially because there is misbehavior in… that is not punishable, because it's unobservable. Basically, if they decrypt messages early, and then,

373
00:44:15.070 --> 00:44:28.750
Jannik Luhn: yeah, peek and see what happens, and then, depending on that, send additional transactions or something like that. That's, nothing that would be visible from the outside. And, it's also bribable in a way that's potentially,

374
00:44:29.860 --> 00:44:37.790
Jannik Luhn: Trustless, so you could maybe set up some sort of, bribery smart contract.

375
00:44:37.920 --> 00:44:55.720
Jannik Luhn: So, yeah, in general, not too excited about this. And Lucid doesn't really have these issues at the moment, because the key publishers can choose their own scheme, and they can switch very quickly. And also, the key publishers are known entities, and they have reputation.

376
00:44:56.210 --> 00:45:05.729
Jannik Luhn: Which kind of factors in into these attacks. I'd rather trust a small committee that I know, that I know the names of of the people, or the users can basically decide on that.

377
00:45:06.660 --> 00:45:21.439
Jannik Luhn: So I was thinking maybe a compromise that we could do between Lucid, that has this optionality problem that we're trying to solve by enshrining, and enshrining one scheme, would be to, have kind of a whitelist of, of…

378
00:45:21.490 --> 00:45:37.059
Jannik Luhn: key publishers. Basically, do Lucid as it is, but whitelist the set of key publishers, so that… and only key publishers who are trusted to release their keys would, yeah, would get under that whitelist.

379
00:45:37.350 --> 00:45:46.789
Jannik Luhn: And now the main question is, of course, is how to come up with the whitelist, and one solution might be to let the validators vote on that list.

380
00:45:47.300 --> 00:46:00.029
Jannik Luhn: Which is… I think, should work. It's maybe a bit unusual in Ethereum, but there is already a little bit of voting by validators, one on the block S limit.

381
00:46:00.430 --> 00:46:17.809
Jannik Luhn: which maybe is just one parameter, so it's maybe much easier to decide on it. But also, arguably, they vote on the set of block builders of the protocol, but that's maybe a different decision. But yeah, just wanted to put that out there and, yeah, see if you guys have thoughts about it.

382
00:46:23.170 --> 00:46:28.969
Jan Bormet: So it's basically Lucid plus, there's, like, kind of a restriction which services can be used, right?

383
00:46:29.480 --> 00:46:30.360
Jannik Luhn: Exactly.

384
00:46:32.870 --> 00:46:47.670
Gottfried Herold: I mean, and you would use this Lucid with an actual threshold scheme, or just a sort of a prescribed set of decryptors, and, I mean, some of them may be threshold, or maybe multiple ones, and some of them are thresholdized, some of them are different, or what.

385
00:46:48.540 --> 00:47:00.520
Jannik Luhn: Yeah, it wouldn't be prescribed by the protocol, but the validators would only accept entities that they think will release their keys, which basically means some sort of threshold committee, I would assume.

386
00:47:05.370 --> 00:47:10.030
Justin Florentine (Besu): Is this a new role that we would be enshrining? It sounds to me like we could take an approach…

387
00:47:10.420 --> 00:47:17.809
Justin Florentine (Besu): out of the ePBS book, right? Where we introduced builders as a new staked entity, that…

388
00:47:17.960 --> 00:47:21.110
Justin Florentine (Besu): You know, had responsibilities and could be punished.

389
00:47:21.750 --> 00:47:26.990
Justin Florentine (Besu): Would that… be necessary.

390
00:47:31.180 --> 00:47:38.629
Jannik Luhn: I'm not sure. I guess in Lucid there is already a little bit of punishment. I'm not sure how easy it is to add additional ones.

391
00:47:43.480 --> 00:47:55.700
Jannik Luhn: I guess you could think of letting the Valley dataset, like, socially slash the key publisher initials, but that probably opens up a different kind of rooms. It's even more difficult.

392
00:48:04.590 --> 00:48:24.789
Gottfried Herold: I mean, my main concern about this is more like, sort of that… a certain set of, sort of entities who provide such a service, which might be some threshold committees, maybe it's even somebody who just says, hey, I will publish, sort of, your keys, trust me, and I'm just a single entity, I just won't steal your data. Trust me, basically something like a trusted broker.

393
00:48:26.360 --> 00:48:39.920
Gottfried Herold: which, I mean, from the protocol's point of view, it's okay. If you use it as a user, that's your problem. It's just a question whether you want to protect the user, or whether you want to protect the protocol. You have to make a bit of a distinction, because this kind of…

394
00:48:39.920 --> 00:48:52.429
Gottfried Herold: And trust mechanism is actually supposed to protect a protocol, and not the user who's actually sending the encrypted transaction. So, different… so you could actually have some…

395
00:48:52.740 --> 00:48:58.779
Gottfried Herold: Multiple entities with different trust relationships to the actual user who uses their service.

396
00:48:58.820 --> 00:49:16.029
Gottfried Herold: My main concern here would be that, I mean, if you sort of have this, and I mean, it's actually, actually, they get fees from their users who use their service, it will probably lead to certain entities, starting up such a service, then maybe they sort of…

397
00:49:16.030 --> 00:49:33.339
Gottfried Herold: get voted in by the protocol, by the other validators, but at some point, you might have some kind of ossification process going on here, and it will be very difficult for new entities, for newcomers, to sort of make a better scheme, maybe a better threshold encryption scheme or whatnot, to actually,

398
00:49:34.290 --> 00:49:44.670
Gottfried Herold: get in into their scheme, because basically the protocol or the validators vote for a sort of a curated list, and now if you're sort of are a newcomer, I mean, you have to basically

399
00:49:44.670 --> 00:49:58.599
Gottfried Herold: socially reach out to this whole set of validators, hey, please add me to the list, and I am good, and I'm a good guy, but, I mean, you cannot really show that you're a good guy, because nobody can use your services until actually the other people have voted on it.

400
00:49:58.630 --> 00:50:02.780
Gottfried Herold: So, you've actually run into a bit of a chicken and egg problem here.

401
00:50:05.720 --> 00:50:12.949
Anders Elowsson: So, one thing we can say about this is that we can always allow,

402
00:50:13.650 --> 00:50:19.169
Anders Elowsson: These non-whitelisted entities to go after the whitelisted entities.

403
00:50:19.600 --> 00:50:25.919
Anders Elowsson: In this case. But I still think that the idea as such might be interested.

404
00:50:26.190 --> 00:50:33.719
Anders Elowsson: Well, being interesting is sort of… you have to be wary about it being sort of a stratum for cartridization within.

405
00:50:33.720 --> 00:50:34.360
Gottfried Herold: the…

406
00:50:34.540 --> 00:50:40.040
Anders Elowsson: the valid data set, so that with this mechanism, we would sort of

407
00:50:40.340 --> 00:50:52.190
Anders Elowsson: over time, perhaps, you know, compromise… like, the big overhanging risk is that we compromise Ethereum as a whole, because we bring this pressure

408
00:50:52.360 --> 00:50:59.249
Anders Elowsson: for… for validators to sync with each other and cartilize with each other. And, and, and, and…

409
00:50:59.390 --> 00:51:11.260
Anders Elowsson: This becomes sort of a, like, a brewing ground for them to start and collaborate with each other and decide between each other who they're going to vote for.

410
00:51:11.700 --> 00:51:22.780
Anders Elowsson: So that's something that… if we… if I compare it to the previous version of this Jannik that you had in the ERP,

411
00:51:22.960 --> 00:51:37.629
Anders Elowsson: Where the transactors instead indicate which entities they trust. In this way, in such a mechanism, we are sort of safeguarding the protocol from this whole game theory in a way that felt

412
00:51:37.810 --> 00:51:40.570
Anders Elowsson: More safe from that one perspective.

413
00:51:42.500 --> 00:51:56.589
Gottfried Herold: Yeah, and I'm not sure to what extent the validators actually have to coordinate, because, I mean… I mean, the validators would not have to vote on the list of allowed entities, but you could have a separate vote for each potential entity candidate.

414
00:51:56.590 --> 00:52:03.010
Gottfried Herold: So, there seems not much, because it's basically a… it's basically an individual vote for each

415
00:52:03.010 --> 00:52:07.570
Gottfried Herold: Candidate, so there's not much coordination, actually, that is needed.

416
00:52:09.140 --> 00:52:09.910
Gottfried Herold: I don't need to.

417
00:52:09.910 --> 00:52:11.760
Anders Elowsson: Or they have to… I mean…

418
00:52:11.910 --> 00:52:14.290
Anders Elowsson: If you are just at the thresholds.

419
00:52:18.960 --> 00:52:34.810
Gottfried Herold: I mean, I would envision this, basically, to be that, basically, validators can sort of send out a message, hey, I want this validator to be added to the trusted list. They can send out a message.

420
00:52:34.830 --> 00:52:51.639
Gottfried Herold: It doesn't even have to go on-chain, and basically, once such a service wants to basically be included into this list, they basically have to provide a special message where they basically produce sufficiently many signed messages from sufficiently many aggregators to be included into this list.

421
00:52:51.790 --> 00:53:03.399
Gottfried Herold: And in particular, there is no… not much, well, coordination issue. Basically, the one who needs to coordinate is that the… the new entity has to basically reach out to each validator.

422
00:53:04.080 --> 00:53:12.329
Gottfried Herold: But not, that the validators don't actually have to talk with each other. At least on the… I mean, at least from a… as far as the mechanism is concerned.

423
00:53:16.820 --> 00:53:20.380
Anders Elowsson: Yeah, but I mean, it's just the…

424
00:53:21.620 --> 00:53:22.750
Gottfried Herold: I, I, I agree that.

425
00:53:22.750 --> 00:53:23.389
Anders Elowsson: Yeah, I…

426
00:53:23.390 --> 00:53:24.890
Gottfried Herold: Dangerous, totally agree.

427
00:53:24.890 --> 00:53:25.410
Anders Elowsson: that.

428
00:53:27.270 --> 00:53:30.029
Anders Elowsson: I think that, that's still,

429
00:53:30.940 --> 00:53:38.640
Anders Elowsson: I don't know, you give the validators this… This option to…

430
00:53:39.220 --> 00:53:43.789
Anders Elowsson: Bring profit to various entities outside of the mechanism, and then

431
00:53:44.010 --> 00:53:47.880
Anders Elowsson: Do you need a minority of the validators to vote in a certain direction? I don't know.

432
00:53:48.290 --> 00:53:48.889
Anders Elowsson: I used to…

433
00:53:48.890 --> 00:53:49.230
Gottfried Herold: I mean.

434
00:53:49.230 --> 00:53:55.090
Anders Elowsson: bit… like, I like the idea, it's innerative, but I've still felt a little bit wary.

435
00:53:55.090 --> 00:54:08.470
Gottfried Herold: Yeah, I'm just saying that, sort of, compared to other, sort of, similar proposals where also financial incentives, like, I don't know, trying to, sort of, direct, rewards to, to research, I mean, it's a bit different, because, sort of, the…

436
00:54:08.820 --> 00:54:16.169
Gottfried Herold: the options to vote for are not… are not pooled. That makes it easier from a mechanism design point of view.

437
00:54:16.690 --> 00:54:17.880
Jan Bormet: Can you repeat me?

438
00:54:17.880 --> 00:54:18.490
Gottfried Herold: Agree.

439
00:54:18.960 --> 00:54:27.600
Jan Bormet: Can you remind me why this optionality actually is such a big deal? Can I not just… let's say there's, like, say, some exchange or something, there's some trading going on, can I not just…

440
00:54:28.280 --> 00:54:31.210
Jan Bormet: let's say I want to do front-running or something.

441
00:54:31.460 --> 00:54:34.759
Jan Bormet: So, Anil, because I want to…

442
00:54:35.280 --> 00:54:36.230
Gottfried Herold: The problem…

443
00:54:36.230 --> 00:54:38.090
Jan Bormet: I mean.

444
00:54:38.090 --> 00:54:44.730
Gottfried Herold: The problem with the optionality is that I can sort of exercise my optionality or not after I know other encrypted transaction.

445
00:54:44.730 --> 00:54:56.510
Jan Bormet: Yeah, after you know transaction after you, right? I think the one before is not so bad, because anybody can observe the state and, like, revert the transaction, right? But the after ones that are bad, that I didn't think about.

446
00:54:56.510 --> 00:55:09.180
Gottfried Herold: Yeah, exactly. That's the reason why, sort of, this other idea by Jannik, that they basically make some ordering that basically your encrypted transactions come with ordering constraints, would also work, but sort of has very bad UX.

447
00:55:18.780 --> 00:55:27.719
Jan Bormet: Is there some, like… is there some, let's say, data of how many transactions per block or per e-book would be currently affected, or, like.

448
00:55:28.690 --> 00:55:31.000
Jan Bormet: With current Layer 1 activity?

449
00:55:31.970 --> 00:55:42.610
Gottfried Herold: very hard to say, because, I mean, to be fair, if we had such a feature, maybe we would see more usage, so there's not… it's absolutely unclear how big the induced demand is.

450
00:55:43.950 --> 00:55:54.820
Jan Bormet: And what is the current demand? Like, what is the current, without the feature? Essentially, does it mean that trading on… I mean, you could argue, okay, trading on Layer 1 is not happening because there can be an MEV, right?

451
00:55:57.380 --> 00:56:00.810
Jan Bormet: But it's not much, because I don't know the actual number, right? It's not too much.

452
00:56:01.780 --> 00:56:04.280
Jan Bormet: Layer 1 MEV right now, or is there?

453
00:56:05.440 --> 00:56:12.009
Justin Florentine (Besu): It's… it's hard to detect. I think our number's around 65 million a year.

454
00:56:12.720 --> 00:56:14.090
Jan Bormet: Okay.

455
00:56:14.090 --> 00:56:19.240
Justin Florentine (Besu): Well, I would say in losses, if you look at it from the other.

456
00:56:26.130 --> 00:56:34.419
Gottfried Herold: And, of course, I mean, as I said, I mean, the big trades… the big, sort of, vulnerable trades are probably not even settled by a L1 because of that reason, so…

457
00:56:34.970 --> 00:56:35.410
Justin Florentine (Besu): Exactly.

458
00:56:36.520 --> 00:56:46.629
Loring: Yeah, I think the demand is probably most represented, by the volume passing through private Mempools rather than through the public Mempool.

459
00:56:52.470 --> 00:57:03.000
Jan Bormet: Because then, if we, like, introduce a Mempool now, and then the scaling, like, we just move the bottleneck from MEV to scaling, then, I mean, you pay the cost some way or another, right?

460
00:57:03.430 --> 00:57:10.700
Jan Bormet: The only way, like, you need to look at the extraction site then. Like, maybe you say if this… if you have an MEP protocol, the extraction is kind of more fair?

461
00:57:11.110 --> 00:57:12.620
Jan Bormet: And not so centralized.

462
00:57:13.320 --> 00:57:16.590
Jan Bormet: Like, because the fees have to be larger, right, and so on, then…

463
00:57:27.420 --> 00:57:30.090
Justin Florentine (Besu): Okay, we are, go ahead.

464
00:57:32.300 --> 00:57:35.369
Anders Elowsson: Yeah, I think one thing that could be interesting for

465
00:57:35.560 --> 00:57:40.340
Anders Elowsson: Jannik, if you wanted to look into it, is perhaps…

466
00:57:40.870 --> 00:57:44.459
Anders Elowsson: Like, some sort of assumption about if we do this

467
00:57:44.610 --> 00:57:50.170
Anders Elowsson: Trust… trust assumption, but on the… At the transaction level instead.

468
00:57:51.940 --> 00:58:00.370
Anders Elowsson: might it look like? We haven't… we… we are worried about various things, and we are worried about how it can be implemented, etc, and what… what

469
00:58:00.580 --> 00:58:13.689
Anders Elowsson: How it will lead to some entities monopolysing, but maybe it can be analyzed a little bit deeper, and like, how will the builder… builder will construct a set based on the options it has available to it?

470
00:58:14.130 --> 00:58:17.490
Anders Elowsson: I don't know. Maybe there's something there to… to think about.

471
00:58:20.650 --> 00:58:24.079
Jannik Luhn: Yeah, I will definitely think about it a little bit more.

472
00:58:24.840 --> 00:58:32.190
Jannik Luhn: But yeah, I see kind of the main argument against it, that it's… Yep.

473
00:58:32.470 --> 00:58:35.319
